<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Wuchale</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Treaty of Ucciale</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Wuchale was signed on 2 May 1889 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It recognised Italian control of the Eritrean highlands, but its Italian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a> texts differed over Article 17, which Italy read as making Ethiopia an Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>. Menelik rejected this reading and denounced the treaty, leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-italo-ethiopian-war">First Italo-Ethiopian War</a> and Italy's defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a> in 1896.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The treaty was signed in the small Ethiopian town of Wuchale by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a>, King of Shewa acting as Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, and Count Pietro Antonelli for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, after the Italian occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>. European colonisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> was expanding fast, and Italy, which already held large territories in Eritrea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, wished to extend its rule to Ethiopia. Menelik resisted this and agreed to a treaty instead, yielding some territory in return for assurances of Ethiopia's independence and for financial and military help from Italy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty had twenty articles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a> and Italian. It declared peace between the two monarchs and their successors, allowed each to appoint consuls in the other's lands and drew a boundary in the highlands, leaving Segheneiti and other highland places to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The monastery of Debra Bizen stayed under Ethiopian control but could not be used for military purposes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> paid an eight per cent duty on goods through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a> but could move its army through the port free of charge. Other articles allowed trade and travel, guaranteed freedom of religion, dealt with disputes, inheritance, crime and extradition, and gave the emperor the right to act against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, which Menelik opposed.</p><p>Article 17 differed between the two texts. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a> it said the King of Kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> could use the Italian government for all business with other powers, leaving him a choice; the Italian text obliged Ethiopia to conduct its foreign affairs through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, in effect making it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>. The Italians attributed the difference to the mistranslation of a verb, permissive in one language and mandatory in the other.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In October 1889 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> told the European governments that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> was now its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> and that they could not deal with it directly. All accepted the claim except the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, which still claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, which disliked an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> nation being subjected to a Catholic one. Menelik rejected Italian protection and denounced the treaty, and Italy invaded in 1895. The war ended with Ethiopia's victory after a two-day battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a>, which preserved its independence, and with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-addis-ababa">Treaty of Addis Ababa</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Wuchale” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Wuchale</b> with Menelik II in 1889 recognised its gains, and on 1 January 1890 the colony of Eritrea was proclaimed.</blockquote><a href="/state/italian-eritrea">Italian Eritrea</a></li></ul></section>
